    "q-174373": {
      "dom_id": "step-q174373",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA=\\{2n∣n\\in N\\},\\,B=\\{3n∣n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A=\\{2,4,6,8,…\\}$\n\nII. $A\\cap B=∅$\n\nIII. $A$ and $B$ are disjoint",
      "worked_solution": "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA\\cap B=∅\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\{2n∣n\\in N\\} \\\\  & =\\{2,4,6,8,…\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =\\{3n∣n\\in N\\} \\\\  & =\\{3,6,9,12,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the intersection is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cap B & =\\{2,4,6,8,…\\}\\cap \\{3,6,9,12\\} \\\\  & =\\{6,12,…\\} \\\\  & \\ne ∅.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the intersection is not empty, the two sets are not disjoint.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
